that i celebrate  is  the reverie  the  intoxication  the cognitive slip and slide the  disinhibition the jazz  of creative flow  creative flow  is musical creative flow  is improvisation creative flow  is the territory beyond the maps it's as  bodhi said in point break where you lose  yourself  and you find yourself  it's the zone it's the surfer  in the pocket as timothy leary said in  these moments your future is right in  front of you  your past is exploding behind you your  footprints are being washed from the  shore it's a non-depletive  non-extractive act done solely for the  pleasure of the act itself and that is  the destiny of man  i mean it feels almost like creative  flow gives us a glimpse of our god-like  possibilities abraham maslow the  positive psychology says we get a thrill  from the god-like possibilities we see  in ourselves in those moments when it  counts on the stage yet we  simultaneously shiver at those very same  possibilities  now i would argue that one of the things  i most enjoy when you and i  engage creatively is that you're so  free now i i don't even think it comes  from training it's just you're born with  this capacity like an actor to like let  go  right to just surrender  but that's not that's not easy for  people right most men lead lives of  quiet desperation people are in a state  of inhibition and anxiousness we're like  clenching all the time and it like  sometimes it just takes like a goddamn  onslaught an initiation a kind of leap  of faith and ego death a trust fall but  that kind of surrender is fundamentally  absurd it makes no sense to leap from  the abyss in a state of trust and grace  i mean that's the definition of paradox  it's insane but in some sense  surrendering our affordances is required  for self-transcendence and for actual  freedom the jazz musician in the pocket  has to have transcended himself at some  point like the end of the film whiplash  like there's gotta be a moment a life  and death psychological  in the blissful crucifixion where you  fully transcend yourself but  self-transcendence is also annihilation  quaking annihilating glory but it is  annihilation and as you know neurotic  people like me we fear death  and so we fumble and stumble trying to  transcend ourselves but mostly bringing  ourselves along kicking and screaming  that ego that just can't unclench and it  can't let go  and perhaps only in psycho psychedelic  visionary states can people experience  themselves without their own inhibiting  ego but others can find those spaces on  stages the artist in the pocket the jazz  musician in the zone the surfer catching  that wave my question to you is simply  this my friend  how do you so easily tap into that from  an  improvisational acting performance  standpoint because you do it well  sometimes i get there filming  you like i lose myself  i become self i try i self transcend  in when i'm recording something but  but when i'm actually  on camera there's it's a little bit  harder because the  self consciousness is in some sense  imposed you know duality is is imposed  by the camera now i am aware of how i'm  being seen but  the the real gift is when that jazz  musician can transcend himself in spite  of being observed the actor can  transcend himself in spite of the camera  being there that he can transport  himself so completely that he forgets  himself and he forgets the camera and he  believes that he is that character  and that that kind of exorcism that  self-possession that he's taken over we  call that a kind of madness but it's  also divine christian bale is a great  example of an actor that can so  self-transcend that can so lose himself  method actors are the same that it is a  kind of cultivated  steering of a schizoid kind of  self-separation you get past yourself to  connect to something deeper in yourself  and  that  watching that is just  it's  wondrous when we see actors do it it's  incredible when we see surfers do it i  think all of them would say man i don't  remember what i did i don't remember i  wasn't there and that's just incredible  so what do you think about that  because it seems easy for you i'm  struggling i'm always struggling

